// Formative practice quiz for protected-pages-and-role-based-screens. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design. First draft, not yet reviewed.
$CATEGORY: react/auth-and-review/protected-pages-and-role-based-screens

::protected-pages-and-role-based-screens o1 - navigate element::In a route guard, how do you redirect a signed-out user while rendering?{
=Return the Navigate component with to set to /login#Correct. Navigate redirects when it is rendered.
~Call navigate directly in the component body#Not quite. Navigation is a side effect and should not happen during render.
~Set window.location during render#Not quite. That is a full page load, and it is a side effect during render.
~Render nothing, and hope they click a link#Not quite. They need to be taken to the login page.
}

::protected-pages-and-role-based-screens o1 - replace on redirect::Why use replace on a redirect to the login page?{
=So Back does not return to the guarded page and redirect again#Correct. Replace keeps the guarded URL out of the history.
~So the redirect is faster#Not quite. It changes history, not speed.
~So the login page is cached#Not quite. Replace has nothing to do with caching.
~Because Navigate needs it to work#Not quite. It works without it, but Back behaves worse.
}

::protected-pages-and-role-based-screens o2 - role-based control::A Publish button should appear only for editors. What is the simplest code?{
=A logical and expression comparing user.role with EDITOR, followed by the button#Correct. It renders the button, or nothing, based on the role.
~A CSS class that hides the button for everyone else#Not quite. The element stays in the page, and it is easily revealed.
~Two separate apps#Not quite. A condition is far simpler.
~A route guard around the button#Not quite. Guards are for routes. A condition handles a single control.
}

::protected-pages-and-role-based-screens o3 - client only::Why is a front-end role check not enough to protect an admin endpoint?{
=Anyone can send requests directly, and browser code can be changed#Correct. Only the server can enforce the rule.
~React does not support role checks#Not quite. It does, but only as interface logic.
~Hidden buttons still appear on phones#Not quite. The issue is direct requests, not screen size.
~It is enough, as long as the code is minified#Not quite. Minified code can still be read and changed.
}
